# Mobile Dinners — configuration
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values. .env.local is
# gitignored; never commit a secret key, and never paste one into a chat.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# The app runs without any of these: payments fall back to a local sandbox
# processor and one-time codes print on screen. Both are refused in production.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- payments
# Stripe Connect (Express). From https://dashboard.stripe.com/apikeys
# Use the TEST keys (sk_test_… / pk_test_…) until you are ready to take money.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# From https://dashboard.stripe.com/webhooks after adding an endpoint at
#   https://<your-domain>/api/payments/webhook
# Subscribe to: payment_intent.succeeded, payment_intent.payment_failed,
#               charge.refunded, account.updated
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- secrets
# 32-byte hex key used to seal POS access tokens at rest. Required in
# production — the app refuses to start without it rather than encrypt with a
# key that is published in the source. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
MD_TOKEN_KEY=

# -------------------------------------------------------------- deployment
# Public origin, used to build Stripe onboarding return links.
MD_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3100

# Where the SQLite database lives. Point this at a mounted volume in production.
MD_DATA_DIR=

# Set to true to allow the demo restaurants and their shared password to be
# seeded in production. Leave unset for a real deployment.
MD_ALLOW_DEMO_SEED=

# Password for admin@mobiledinners.com, the platform administrator.
#
# That address is ALWAYS a platform administrator — it is hard-coded, and this
# repository is public, so the address is known. Nothing protects the account
# except this password, so it is not optional theatre:
#
#   · set it, and the app creates or updates the account on every boot. That is
#     how the account comes to exist on a host with no shell, like Render.
#   · rotating this variable and redeploying rotates the password.
#   · leave it unset and the account is never created, which is the safe
#     default. Nobody can sign in as an administrator at all.
#   · the demo password and anything under 12 characters are refused outright.
#
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(18).toString('base64url'))"
MD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Additional platform administrators, comma separated. admin@mobiledinners.com
# does not need to appear here. With this unset and no platform admin account,
# production has no administrators at all — which is deliberate.
MD_ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Minutes a STAFF session may sit untouched before it ends. Default 30.
# Set to 0 to switch the idle timeout off entirely.
#
# The session cookie already dies when the browser closes. This covers the
# browsers that put it back — Chrome's "continue where you left off", and the
# phone browsers that never really quit — so a terminal closed at the end of a
# shift is not still signed in as that person the next morning.
#
# A kitchen display holding its event stream open counts as activity, so a
# mounted screen does not sign itself out during a quiet hour. Diners are not
# affected: nobody shares a phone the way a kitchen shares a terminal.
MD_STAFF_IDLE_MINUTES=

# Phone numbers that accept a fixed one-time code, comma separated as
# NUMBER:CODE. Twilio A2P 10DLC registration takes one to three weeks, and
# until it clears a deployed site mints codes and sends them nowhere, so
# nobody can sign in and no order can be placed. This pins a code to numbers
# you choose so the consumer flow can be tested.
#
# It is not a bypass: the number must be listed here, and the code still goes
# through the same hash, expiry, attempt cap and rate limit as any other.
# Use 555-01xx numbers, which are reserved and cannot belong to anyone.
# REMOVE THIS once real SMS is live.
#   MD_OTP_TEST_NUMBERS=+15555550100:424242,+15555550101:314159
MD_OTP_TEST_NUMBERS=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- sms
# Twilio Programmable SMS, used to send customer sign-in codes.
#
# With all three set, one-time codes are sent for real and are never returned
# in the response. With any of them missing, nothing is sent — which is why
# MD_OTP_TEST_NUMBERS above exists, and why a deployed site is untransactable
# until either that or these are configured.
#
# From https://console.twilio.com — Account SID and Auth Token are on the
# dashboard. Rotate the auth token if it is ever pasted anywhere.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Either a number you own in E.164 (+15551234567) OR a Messaging Service SID
# starting MG. Prefer the Messaging Service: A2P 10DLC attaches its campaign to
# a service, not to a bare number, so that is what registration expects.
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=

# Before this works in the US you must register for A2P 10DLC (long code,
# typically one to three weeks) or complete toll-free verification (usually
# faster). Unregistered application traffic to US numbers is filtered by the
# carriers, so the API returns success and nothing arrives.
#
# Also worth doing in the console, with no code: Messaging → Geo permissions,
# and allow only the countries you actually serve. SMS pumping fraud bills you
# for premium international traffic, and that setting is what kills it.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- maps
# MapTiler, for the tiles on the delivery tracking map. Free tier, no card:
#   https://cloud.maptiler.com/account/keys/
#
# PUBLIC by design — a tile key is sent to every browser that loads a map, and
# no amount of care changes that. Protect it by restricting the key to your
# domains under "Allowed origins" in the MapTiler dashboard; that is the
# control that actually works.
#
# Without it the map area explains that no provider is configured rather than
# rendering an empty grey box.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPTILER_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------- not yet implemented
# Listed so the shape is known; nothing reads these today.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# MAPS_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- delivery
# DoorDash Drive — white-label courier network. Not the DoorDash marketplace:
# no listing, no commission, and the diner never leaves our app.
# From https://developer.doordash.com/portal — create a Drive credential set.
DOORDASH_DEVELOPER_ID=
DOORDASH_KEY_ID=
DOORDASH_SIGNING_SECRET=

# Signs inbound status updates. Add a webhook in the portal pointing at
#   https://<your-domain>/api/delivery/webhook
DOORDASH_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Set to "sandbox" while testing so the ops dashboard says so out loud.
DOORDASH_ENV=sandbox
